WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:12.617
Earlier in this lesson, one cook kept five pots going on one thread: five waits of 200 milliseconds, 358 milliseconds in all.

2
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.676
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

3
00:00:15.676 --> 00:00:18.082
A function's locals live with its thread.

4
00:00:18.082 --> 00:00:22.015
If the function pauses, and the thread walks off, where do they go?

5
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:32.016
A running function has a frame on its thread's stack: its local variables, and the line it's up to.

6
00:00:32.016 --> 00:00:33.491
That's the cook's head.

7
00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:35.415
Onions are in, simmer is next.

8
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.383
Now she walks over to the salad, and her head fills up with lettuce.

9
00:00:39.383 --> 00:00:42.888
Ask her how much salt went into the soup, and she'll say: some.

10
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:47.116
A thread is the same: the next code it runs reuses that memory.

11
00:00:47.116 --> 00:00:51.165
So a pausing function must write its place down somewhere else.

12
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.025
One fix is to never walk off.

13
00:00:54.025 --> 00:00:59.699
That's Thread.sleep, the cook standing at the pot, which is what we came here to avoid.

14
00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:06.874
Another fix: copy the whole stack, like the order slip that Java's virtual threads clip to the board.

15
00:01:06.874 --> 00:01:12.423
That needs the JVM's help, and Kotlin coroutines run on any JVM, and on Android.

16
00:01:12.423 --> 00:01:15.545
So the compiler writes the place down itself.

17
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:17.873
The way a good cook does.

18
00:01:17.873 --> 00:01:25.384
She puts a bookmark at the next step, sticks a note on the page, onions: measured, sets the kitchen timer, and walks away.

19
00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:30.433
When it rings, she opens the book at the bookmark, reads the note, and carries on.

20
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.431
Let's map it.

21
00:01:31.431 --> 00:01:33.605
The recipe is the function's code.

22
00:01:33.605 --> 00:01:35.971
The bookmark says which step is next.

23
00:01:35.971 --> 00:01:37.634
The note holds the locals.

24
00:01:37.634 --> 00:01:41.087
And the cook is the thread, free while the book waits.

25
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.021
Let's shrink it to one function.

26
00:01:44.021 --> 00:01:46.990
makeSauce waits for fryOnions, then for simmer.

27
00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:51.791
Each call that may pause is called a suspension point, and this one has two.

28
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:55.410
First, the compiler quietly changes the function.

29
00:01:55.410 --> 00:02:05.344
javap, a JDK tool that prints what's inside a compiled class, shows makeSauce with one more parameter, a Continuation, and returning Object.

30
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:15.204
Object, because it now returns one of two things: the real sauce, or a marker, COROUTINE_SUSPENDED, which means: I've paused, I'll call you back.

31
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.609
A three line function grew a parameter nobody wrote.

32
00:02:18.609 --> 00:02:21.386
If a teammate did that, you'd have questions.

33
00:02:21.386 --> 00:02:25.767
Here, it's the trick: the hidden parameter is how the bookmark travels.

34
00:02:26.733 --> 00:02:30.599
Next, the compiler cuts the body at each suspension point.

35
00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:31.799
makeSauce has two.

36
00:02:31.799 --> 00:02:33.665
How many steps will it make?

37
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:40.358
Three: up to fryOnions, up to simmer, and after simmer.

38
00:02:40.358 --> 00:02:42.506
A label numbers them 0, 1 and 2.

39
00:02:42.506 --> 00:02:47.673
Code with a counter like that, saying which step is next, is a state machine.

40
00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:58.519
The bookmark is one small object the compiler writes for this function: an int called label, for the page, and two fields, L$0 and L$1, for the sticky note.

41
00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:00.301
Let's follow one call.

42
00:03:00.301 --> 00:03:10.179
The label is 0, so step 0 writes the onions on the note, in L$0, sets the label to 1, and calls fryOnions, handing over the bookmark.

43
00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:16.883
fryOnions has to wait, so it returns COROUTINE_SUSPENDED, and makeSauce passes the marker straight up.

44
00:03:16.883 --> 00:03:20.672
The cook's hands are empty, and she goes off to other work.

45
00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.591
Notice what's left: no thread, no frame.

46
00:03:23.591 --> 00:03:26.384
Just the bookmark, an object on the heap.

47
00:03:26.533 --> 00:03:32.326
When the onions are golden, something calls resumeWith on the bookmark, with the golden onions.

48
00:03:32.326 --> 00:03:36.595
That calls makeSauce again, and a switch on the label jumps to step 1.

49
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:42.809
Step 1 writes the golden onions on the note, in L$1, sets the label to 2, and calls simmer.

50
00:03:42.809 --> 00:03:45.813
simmer waits too, so the thread leaves again.

51
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.452
When simmer answers, resumeWith runs once more, and step 2 returns the sauce to whoever was waiting: sauce from golden onions.

52
00:03:54.452 --> 00:03:56.061
Nobody stood at the pan.

53
00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.707
makeSauce paused twice.

54
00:03:57.707 --> 00:04:00.395
So how many bookmark objects did it make?

55
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.120
One, made when the call starts, and reused at every pause.

56
00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.365
Only the label and the note change.

57
00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:11.200
Now the names.

58
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.265
The bookmark is a continuation: it knows how to carry on a paused coroutine.

59
00:04:16.265 --> 00:04:22.596
It has a context, the coroutine's settings, and resumeWith: the wait is over, here's the value.

60
00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:28.656
Passing what to do next as a hidden parameter is called continuation-passing style.

61
00:04:28.656 --> 00:04:32.795
And javap agrees: one switch over the label, cases 0 to 2.

62
00:04:33.533 --> 00:04:38.975
Compare the slip on the board, where the JVM copied every frame of a virtual thread.

63
00:04:38.975 --> 00:04:45.323
A coroutine keeps one small bookmark per suspend function still running, with only its own fields.

64
00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:50.169
That's why a paused coroutine is cheap: you can have hundreds of thousands.

65
00:04:50.169 --> 00:04:55.059
And plain code can't call a suspend function: it has no bookmark to hand over.

66
00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.357
Here the picture breaks.

67
00:04:57.357 --> 00:05:00.859
A real cook hears the timer and walks back by herself.

68
00:05:00.859 --> 00:05:08.577
A paused coroutine does nothing until something calls resumeWith: the library's timer, or the code that got the answer.

69
00:05:08.733 --> 00:05:12.147
Forget to call it, and it waits forever, very politely.

70
00:05:12.147 --> 00:05:17.237
So somebody has to ring the timer, and the last section shows who, for a callback.

71
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.723
Here's the Kotlin, with Java beside it.

72
00:05:20.723 --> 00:05:23.795
In Kotlin, the compiler writes the bookmark.

73
00:05:23.795 --> 00:05:28.473
In Java, a virtual thread blocks, and the JVM saves the whole slip.

74
00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:32.515
So, where do the locals go when the thread walks off?

75
00:05:32.515 --> 00:05:35.763
Onto a sticky note, in a bookmark the compiler wrote.

76
00:05:35.763 --> 00:05:41.831
A suspended coroutine is a bookmark, not a thread, and something has to call resumeWith to wake it.

77
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:42.862
Quick check.

78
00:05:42.862 --> 00:05:45.090
fryOnions waits once, on delay.

79
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.036
How many steps does the compiler give it?

80
00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:51.445
Two.

81
00:05:51.445 --> 00:05:56.272
Label 0 runs up to the delay, and label 1 after it.

82
00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:58.639
javap shows cases 0 to 1.
